findings.detail:
**What the Dock offers**
The Dock gives almost every civ the same European sequence:
- Galley (Mediterranean) → War Galley → Galleon (Atlantic, 16th century; 53/56);
- Hulk → War Hulk (56/56) → Carrack (48/56);
- Fire Ship (56/56), Demolition Ship and Cannon Galleon;
- Trade Cog, the Hanseatic ship of the 12th–14th centuries.

The game's Hulk is a 'warship with a close range melee attack', but the medieval hulk was a cargo ship.

**The problem**
It is a gameplay necessity, because of water maps, but it produces impossible fleets. Assessment: **abstraction** for the maritime civs and **wrong** for the civs without access to the sea.

**What is right**
There are **faithful** regional ships:
- Longship (Vikings, Danes, Saxons, Varangians);
- Dromon (Byzantines);
- Lou Chuan (the 'tower ships' of the Han navy; Chinese, Jurchens and Three Kingdoms);
- Turtle Ship (Yi Sun-sin, 1592);
- Caravel;
- the Malay Harbor.

The Chola Thirisadai is faithful in concept (the Chola fleet is real), but the attestation of the name is uncertain. Three civs without an oceanic tradition have no Galleon: Aztecs, Khitans and Malians (**plausible**).

**The Catapult Galleon**
It exists in 11 civs: the six American civs, the four Norse civs and the Cumans. It serves as a substitute for the Cannon Galleon for civs without gunpowder. But it is given precisely to peoples without mechanical artillery (the Americas), and not to those who mounted it on ships (Romans, Byzantines). The catapult 'galleon' is **unfounded**; Chronicles mode already has an ancient 'Catapult Ship', which would be the right reference.

**The Wonder (56/56)**
It is described as proof of 'the superiority of your civilization', a rhetoric of civilizational hierarchy typical of the 19th century.
- For civs with a monumental tradition (Hagia Sophia, Angkor, Templo Mayor, Djenné) it is a plausible **abstraction**. Construction times varied a great deal: Hagia Sophia was built in about 5 years (532–537), Angkor Wat in about 30.
- For Tupi, Mapuche, Huns or Cumans (whose monuments were kurgans and stone statues) it is **unfounded**.

**The University (56/56)**
It is the Latin corporation (Bologna, 1088 by tradition). Equivalents exist in other cultures:
- the madrasa (Nizamiyya of Baghdad, 1065–67);
- Nalanda;
- the Chinese Taixue/Guozijian;
- the Văn Miếu–Quốc Tử Giám (1070/1076);
- Sankore, in Timbuktu;
- the Aztec calmecac and the Inca yachaywasi.

But there is no equivalent for every civ. Assessment: a reasonable **abstraction**, with Eurocentric names.

**The solution already exists in the repository**
In Chronicles mode, the University is called Academy (and has Lighthouse) and the Town Center offers Oligarchy, Democracy and Tyranny.

**Different criteria for each civ**
The label 'civilization' covers things of very different kinds:
- **peoples:** Celts, Slavs, Goths, Huns, Tupi (the Tupi text says it is a generic term for several tribes);
- **states or dynasties:** Byzantines, Khmer;
- **factions in a civil war, with trainable heroes:** Shu, Wei and Wu;
- **a language family:** Dravidians, a term popularised by Robert Caldwell (1856) from the Sanskrit drāviḍa;
- **regions:** Bengalis and Hindustanis (the latter is a Persian term of the period itself, plausible);
- **an activity:** Vikings (víkingr = raiding expedition);
- **modern nations:** Italians, Spanish.

The 'Chinese' cover nearly a millennium in a single civ (the text runs from the Sui, 581, to the Ming), while 3rd-century China has three.

**Exonyms**
- 'Saracens' is a Graeco-Latin exonym (Sarakēnoi, already in Antiquity), generalised by medieval Christians; the game's own text explains its origin.
- 'Byzantines' was popularised by Hieronymus Wolf (1557). They called themselves Rhomaioi, which is odd now that there is a separate 'Romans' civ.
- 'Celts' applies a modern linguistic label (Edward Lhuyd, 1707) to the medieval Scots, Irish and Welsh.

**Assessment**
**Abstraction** or convention. But it has consequences: it decides what is removed from and what is added to each civ.

themes.summary:
The 56-civ roster of AoE2 DE does not follow a coherent principle of representation. It is the result of 27 years of expansions (1999–2026), each with its own commercial logic and its own idea of what a “civ” is. I counted the 56 civs in data/data.json using my own regional classification, which is debatable at the margins. There are 22 European civs (39%, or 24 with the Armenians and Georgians), 2 from sub-Saharan Africa, 3 from the Middle East/Iran/Anatolia, none from Oceania and none from America north of Mexico. Of the 29 civs added after African Kingdoms (2015), none is African and ten are European. The latest expansion (The Viking Sagas, commit 3bb43b1, 11 September 2026) brought three more civs from the North Sea and Baltic world: Saxons, Danes and Varangians.

The granularity is inconsistent. The Three Kingdoms take up three slots for about 60 years of the 3rd century, while “Chinese” covers everything from 581 to the Ming dynasty. The Norse world has three civs (Vikings, Danes, Varangians) and the Slavs have one, which in gameplay is mostly the Rus'. The names mix:
- endonyms (Magyars);
- conventional exonyms (Byzantines, Aztecs);
- contentious exonyms (Saracens, Tatars);
- an activity term (Vikings);
- modern scholarly categories (Celts);
- mislabels: “Britons” designates the English, precisely the people who displaced the Britons.

The time span runs from the late 2nd century (Shu/Wei/Wu, whose texts begin in 184) to the 17th century (Winged Hussar, mounted Mapuche). Some unique units belong to another people or another century: the Huskarl for the Goths, the Gbeto (Dahomey) for the Malians, the Woad Raider (Antiquity) for the Celts and the Rocket Cart (Ming/Joseon) for the Khitans. The Arambai and Manipur Cavalry for the Burmese are a more debatable case.

It is fair to acknowledge real progress and reasonable abstractions:
- the split of “Indians” into four civs (2022);
- the coverage of the Caucasus (2023);
- the change to “Maya” and “Inca” (2025);
- the care taken not to give gunpowder to the Romans and the Three Kingdoms;
- recent texts that are much better than those of 1999;
- names such as Malians, Bengalis or Byzantines, which are defensible conventions.

The main problem is not a lack of historical competence. It is the lack of a roster criterion applied retroactively.

I propose:
1. An explicit principle: a civ is a political-cultural sphere with about two centuries of continuity within c. 500–1500, with a non-pejorative name and units from its own people and period.
2. Indicative regional quotas.
3. Mergers and dynastic variants, instead of multiplying slots.
4. Using the “era” and “meta” fields the repository already has (currently “base” and empty for all 56 civs) to document the historical scope of each civ.

findings.detail:
I counted the 56 civs in data/data.json using my own regional classification (debatable at the margins). Europe, including Byzantium, has 22 of the 56 civs (39%). With the Armenians and Georgians it is 24 (43%). With the Huns and Cumans, whose history in the game takes place mostly in Europe, it is 26 (46%). By contrast:
- Africa: 3 civs (Berbers, Malians, Ethiopians), of which 2 are sub-Saharan;
- Middle East, Iran and Anatolia: 3 (Saracens, Persians, Turks);
- Americas: 6, all from Mesoamerica southwards;
- Oceania: 0.

Population estimates for the year 1000 are very uncertain, but the orders of magnitude are enough to show the contrast:
- Scandinavia had perhaps 1 to 2 million inhabitants and has 3 civs;
- sub-Saharan Africa had some tens of millions and many documented states (Ghana, Gao/Songhai, Kanem, Makuria, Alodia, Kilwa, Ife/Benin…) and has 2;
- the British Isles have 3 civs;
- the territory of present-day China has at least 6.

The trend has worsened. Of the 29 civs added after African Kingdoms (2015), none is African and ten are European: Bulgarians, Lithuanians, Burgundians, Sicilians, Bohemians, Poles, Romans, Saxons, Danes and Varangians.

There are understandable reasons for this:
- the market;
- the abundance of European sources in accessible languages;
- the popularity of certain themes, such as the Vikings;
- the real fragmentation of medieval Europe.

But that fragmentation also existed in the Sahel, in India and in South-East Asia. This is not a factual error. It is a representation choice that distorts the picture of the medieval world that the game conveys. Assessment: a structural problem, not a matter of detail.

With The Viking Sagas, the Norse world now has three civs. It is four if you count the legendary Scandinavian origin of the Goths in Jordanes.

The “Vikings” civ loses its own identity. The episodes its text narrates are repeated in other texts:
- Lindisfarne appears in 3 (Vikings, Danes, Saxons);
- the Great Army of 865 in 3 (Vikings, Danes, Britons);
- Cnut in 3 (Vikings, Danes, Saxons);
- 1066 in 4 (Vikings, Saxons, Britons, Varangians).

“Viking” designates an activity, not a people: víkingr is one who goes on víking, a seaborne raiding expedition. Most Scandinavians were farmers. The game's own text says that the name means “men of the north”, which is false (that is “Norsemen”, Normanni). The civ's techs point to Norway: Bogsveigar evokes the archers of the Norwegian sagas, such as Einar Þambarskelfir at Svolder (c. 1000). “Norse” or “Norwegians” would be more coherent.

The Varangian Guard was the guard of the Byzantine emperor. It is traditionally dated to 988, when Vladimir sent 6,000 men to Basil II. In the game it can be trained at the Barracks by Byzantines, Danes, Saxons, Varangians and Vikings. The ethnic link is real: Scandinavians of various origins served in the Guard (Harald Hardrada between 1034 and 1042, Icelanders such as Bolli Bollason) and, after 1066, many Englishmen. The problem lies elsewhere: an institution of the court of Constantinople is turned into heavy infantry that can be recruited in Jutland or Wessex. Assessment: plausible for Byzantines and Varangians, stretched for Danes, Saxons and Vikings.

The Saxons' Longship, on the other hand, is defensible. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records in 896 that Alfred had “langscipu” built against the Danish ships.

The Vikings' text attributes Lindisfarne to “Danes”. This is contested (many historians think of Norwegians), but it reflects the English sources, which called all Scandinavians “Dene”. The text also gives 871 for a two-year siege of Paris, ended with a ransom. That description matches the siege of 885–886. In that siege, Charles the Fat allowed the Vikings to plunder Burgundy, and not western France as the text says.

“Britons” is a labelling error. The Britons were the Brittonic-speaking peoples: the Welsh, Cornish, Cumbrians and the Bretons of Armorica. It was precisely they whom the Anglo-Saxons displaced, and the Britons' own history text explains this before narrating English history. The civ represents Anglo-Norman and Plantagenet England: Longbowman, Yeomen, Warwolf (Edward I's trebuchet at Stirling, 1304), the Hundred Years' War. There is a partial bridge that it is fair to point out. The longbow had a strong Welsh tradition (Gerald of Wales describes the archers of Gwent in 1191) and Edward I recruited Welsh archers en masse. Assessment: wrong name, coherent English content.

“Saxons” is also an imperfect name:
- it ignores the Angles, who gave England its name; from Alfred onwards, the English themselves said Englisc or Angelcynn;
- it collides with the continental Saxons (Widukind and the Ottonian dynasty, 919–1024), whom the game covers with the Teutons;
- “Saxons” was the name that the Welsh and the Gaels gave to the English (Saeson, Sasannach).
With Saxons and Britons, England has two civs, before and after 1066. And the Britons' text already narrated Alfred and the Danelaw.

“Celts” lumps together the Irish, Scots, Welsh and, in the text, Bretons. The label comes from Edward Lhuyd, who applied it to the insular languages in 1707. No ancient source calls the inhabitants of the islands “Celts”, and the medieval Irish and Welsh did not see themselves as such. Simon James (The Atlantic Celts, 1999) and John Collis (2003) question the concept of “Atlantic Celts”. Barry Cunliffe and John Koch argue for continuities. The debate is open. The game's text places the Celts in “northern Europe” and makes them the builders of Stonehenge “5000 years ago”. This is wrong: Stonehenge is Neolithic, long before any Celtic language in Britain.

The Woad Raider unique unit comes from Caesar's ancient Britanni (De Bello Gallico 5.14). The Latin text speaks of vitrum, traditionally read as woad, a contested reading. The unit is probably also inspired by the aesthetics of Braveheart (1995). It does not come from the medieval Scots or Irish, who had kerns (ceithearn) and, from the 13th century, gallowglass (gallóglaigh). Assessment: anachronistic.

The Saxons' text has several errors:
- it assigns Ælle to Essex, when he was king of Sussex;
- it calls Middlesex a kingdom, when it was a province of the East Saxons;
- it says that the Saxons adopted the shield wall from the Vikings, when the bordweall already appears in Old English poetry;
- it presents Alfred as the unifier of England in 886. In that year Alfred took London; unification came with Æthelstan, in 927.

“Malians” is a reasonable name, because it comes from the Mali Empire and not from an exonym. The text covers Ghana (Soninke) and Mali (Mandinka); Songhai appears only as the rival that hastened the decline.

The Gbeto unique unit comes from Dahomey (Fon, present-day Benin, 17th–19th centuries). The gbeto were the elephant huntresses of the female corps associated with the “Agojie”. The unit is wrong in both place and time. For Mali there are direct sources on the army: al-Umari (c. 1337–1340) speaks of about 100,000 men, of whom c. 10,000 were horsemen, and Ibn Battuta was in Mali in 1352–1353. Assessment of the Gbeto: anachronistic and from another people.

The Ethiopians are relatively well grounded (Aksum, Zagwe, Solomonic dynasty). But neighbours with written sources and their own military tradition are missing:
- Christian Nubia (Makuria), which halted the Arab expansion at Dongola (642 and 652) and imposed the Baqt treaty; the Arabs called its archers “archers of the eyes”;
- Kanem-Bornu (c. 9th century–1893), with heavy cavalry in quilted armour and mail, described in the chronicle of Ahmad Furtu (16th century);
- Songhai (1464–1591, defeated at Tondibi);
- the Swahili cities (Kilwa, Mombasa), well described by João de Barros and Duarte Barbosa;
- the Adal Sultanate, which almost destroyed Ethiopia in 1529–1543, in a war in which the Portuguese expedition of Cristóvão da Gama (1541–1543) intervened;
- the Kingdom of Benin and the Kingdom of Kongo, both in contact with Portugal since the 1480s (Diogo Cão in Kongo, 1483; João Afonso de Aveiro in Benin, 1486).

For a Portuguese player, several of these peoples appear in the Portuguese sources themselves. Their absence becomes even more conspicuous now that The Viking Sagas has added three civs from Northern Europe.

Judging by their units, techs and texts, the civs are spread across some 1,500 years.

In Late Antiquity there are:
- Romans (395–476 in the text);
- Huns (370s–469);
- Goths (376–711);
- Persians (text up to the Islamic conquest, 636–651);
- Shu, Wei and Wu (184–280).

In the early modern period there are:
- Winged Hussar (Poles and Lithuanians), at its peak between Kircholm (1605) and Vienna (1683);
- Conquistador and Missionary (Spanish), from 1519 onwards;
- Caravel, Feitoria, Organ Gun, Arquebus and Circumnavigation (Portuguese, 15th–16th centuries);
- Grand Trunk Road (Hindustanis, 1540s);
- Mapuche and Tupi (1540s–1560s).

For a Portuguese player there is a curious detail. Circumnavigation is given to the Portuguese. Ferdinand Magellan was Portuguese, but the expedition (1519–1522, completed by Elcano) belonged to the Crown of Castile. And, in gameplay, medieval Portugal (Afonso Henriques, Aljubarrota in 1385) barely exists. The text covers the Reconquista, but the bonuses and units are from the 15th–16th centuries.

The lack of a period criterion also creates technological anachronisms. The Goths have Hand Cannoneer and Bombard Cannon, while the Huns, from the same era, have no gunpowder unit at all. Huns, Romans, Goths, Shu, Wei and Wu have Galleon.

To be fair, Return of Rome and The Three Kingdoms took care not to give gunpowder to Romans, Shu, Wei and Wu: they have no Hand Cannoneer, Bombard Cannon, Cannon Galleon or Bombard Tower. The repository itself already separates Antiquity. The Chronicles civs have era “antiquity” and were moved to a page of their own (commit b34082d, 28 April 2026). Romans and the Three Kingdoms civs stayed in the main roster, with era “base”.

There is a sharp contrast between the recent texts (Khitans, Muisca, Tupi, Gurjaras), which are careful and have identified sources, and those of 1999.

Aztecs:
- the text says Aztec culture “invented little that was new” and “passed on no significant technology” or ideas. This ignores the chinampas, the calendar, writing, the tributary organisation and the fact that Tenochtitlan was one of the largest cities in the world in 1519;
- it says they had already been devastated by diseases brought by traders, when in fact smallpox arrived in 1520 with the Narváez expedition, in the middle of the conquest;
- it presents the sacrifice figures (“several thousand in a single day”) without the caveats that historians make today.

Maya:
- it states that Maya religion was “relatively simple” and the architecture less developed;
- it attributes the collapse to earthquakes or volcanoes, whereas today droughts, war and political collapse are discussed;
- it speaks of three surviving codices, but there have been four since INAH authenticated the Códice Maya de México (Grolier) in 2018;
- it underestimates the present-day Maya population, which numbers several million in Guatemala and Mexico.

Other old texts:
- Celts: the Stonehenge error and the Celts in “northern Europe”.
- Vikings: it says the name means “men of the north”, that the raids ended at the end of the 10th century (Sweyn Forkbeard, the Battle of Maldon in 991 and Harald Hardrada in 1066 show otherwise) and that Baldwin I of Jerusalem was a Norman (he was from Boulogne).
- Huns: it credits them, albeit with reservations, with inventing the stirrup.
- Goths: it dates Alaric's sack to 409 (it was in 410) and says the Goths were the first to sack Rome (the Gauls did so c. 390 BC).

These texts are part of the representation: they tell the player who was “advanced” and who was “primitive”.

I assessed each people against three criteria: a lasting state or sphere, a distinct military tradition and sufficient sources.

Strong cases:
- Nubians/Makuria (642 to the 14th–15th centuries; Baqt);
- Seljuks (1037–1194; Rum until c. 1308);
- Mamluks (1250–1517);
- Tibetans (618–842);
- Tanguts/Western Xia (1038–1227; heavy cavalry and the poxi of the Song Shi);
- Kanem-Bornu;
- Songhai (1464–1591);
- Purépecha (c. 1300–1530);
- Serbs (Nemanjićs; Velbazhd 1330; Dušan emperor in 1346; Kosovo 1389);
- Siamese (Sukhothai, Ayutthaya);
- Champa.

Medium cases:
- Timurids (better separated from the Tatars);
- Andalusians: Umayyad Córdoba (756–1031), taifas, Nasrids until 1492; currently split between Saracens and Berbers;
- Aragonese/Catalans (Almogavars; Catalan Company, 1303–1311);
- Swiss (from Morgarten, 1315, to Marignano, 1515);
- Wallachians and Moldavians (Posada 1330, Rovine 1395, Vaslui 1475);
- Uyghurs, Khazars, Göktürks, Avars and Khwarazmians (1077–1231);
- Swahili/Kilwa;
- Kingdoms of Benin and Kongo (Portuguese sources from the 1480s onwards; the Benin bronze plaques show warriors);
- Sinhalese and Javanese;
- Chimú;
- Mississippians (mainly archaeological evidence);
- Polynesians. The settlement of East Polynesia and New Zealand takes place c. 1000–1300 (Wilmshurst et al., 2011), and Tonga had a maritime chiefdom. New mechanics would be needed, as in The Last Chieftains.

Weak cases, or better as a variant:
- Venetians and Genoese (already “Italians”);
- Teutonic Order (already the Teutons' UU);
- Old Prussians (known almost only through their enemies, such as Peter of Dusburg, 1326);
- Navarre;
- Albanians (Skanderbeg, 1443–1468, a single generation);
- Delhi Sultanate (already, in effect, the Hindustanis: it is enough to change the name or the description);
- Ghaznavids and Ghurids (Perso-Turkic variant);
- Great Zimbabwe and Monomotapa (monumental, but with little military evidence; they would fit an economic design);
- Mughals and Safavids (early modern period).

A fair critique has to acknowledge the progress made.

In names:
- “Indians”, which was confused with the Amerindian peoples, was split into four regional civs (2022), with interlinked texts;
- “Mayans” and “Incas” became “Maya” and “Inca” (2025), following academic convention.

In coverage:
- Mountain Royals (2023) brought the Christian Caucasus with Armenians and Georgians, including well-identified institutions such as the Fortified Church and the Mule Cart;
- The Last Chieftains brought non-imperial societies and texts that acknowledge the limits of the sources. The Tupi text presents the name as an umbrella term and cites Hans Staden.

In period and units:
- Return of Rome and The Three Kingdoms avoided giving gunpowder to their ancient civs;
- several recent techs have a clear documentary basis: Kshatriyas, Wootz Steel, Tuntian (Cao Cao's military agricultural colonies), Szlachta Privileges, Wagenburg Tactics, Paper Money (Hồ Quý Ly, 1396) and Garland Wars (xochiyaoyotl; the interpretation of the “flower wars” is debated, for example by Ross Hassig);
- the repository itself moved the Antiquity civs (Chronicles) to a page of their own.

The problem is not a lack of historical competence. It is the lack of a roster principle applied retroactively to the 1999–2015 civs.

proposals:
Naming rule, in this order:
1. a recognisable endonym (Maya, Inca, Magyars, Rus');
2. a non-pejorative scholarly convention (Byzantines, with a note on Rhomaioi);
3. never an activity term (Vikings), a contentious exonym (Saracens) or a swapped label (Britons).
Concrete renamings for debate:
- Britons → English;
- Celts → Gaels (Irish and Scots), with “Britons” possibly moving to the Welsh, Cornish and Bretons;
- Saxons → Anglo-Saxons, or a variant of the English;
- Vikings → Norse;
- Slavs → Rus';
- Saracens → Arabs;
- Tatars → Golden Horde, splitting off the Timurids;
- Sicilians → Italo-Normans;
- Aztecs → “Aztecs (Mexica)”.
“Teutons” can stay, because it is plausible.

In a fork of the repository, fill in the “meta” field of each civ, which is currently empty in all 56 entries of data/data.json. Suggested fields:
- endonym;
- exonyms and aliases;
- period covered (start and end);
- states included;
- main sources;
- “known anachronisms” (for example, Goths: Huskarl, Hand Cannoneer, Bombard Cannon).
Then show this data in a “Historical scope” box on the site. It is a small change, to data and interface only, and it would make the site a resource for this kind of debate.

Indicative regional quotas for 56 slots:
- Western and Central Europe: 11;
- Eastern Europe and the Balkans: 5;
- Caucasus: 2;
- Steppe and Central Asia: 4;
- Middle East and North Africa: 6;
- sub-Saharan Africa: 6;
- South Asia: 4;
- East Asia: 6;
- South-East Asia: 5;
- Americas: 6;
- Oceania: 1.
Europe would have 16 to 18 slots (about 30%), still above its demographic weight, but far from the current 39–46%.

Free up slots with mergers and variants instead of creating more civs:
- Shu, Wei and Wu become a single “Three Kingdoms” civ in a Late Antiquity roster;
- Vikings and Danes form “Norse”, with Danish, Norwegian and Swedish-Varangian variants;
- Saxons become a variant of the English;
- Chinese gets Tang, Song and Ming variants;
- Arabs gets Umayyad, Abbasid and Fatimid variants.

Priority of new civs, by strength of case:
- first: Nubians (Makuria), Seljuks, Mamluks (the Mameluke could be shared with the Arabs), Tibetans, Tanguts (who would take the camel-mounted catapult, the poxi of the Song Shi), Kanem-Bornu, Songhai, Purépecha, Serbs, Siamese and Champa;
- next: Andalusians, Timurids, Swahili/Kilwa, Benin/Kongo, Mississippians and Polynesians (the latter with their own mechanics, as in The Last Chieftains).

Reassign misplaced units and techs:
- Huskarl to the Norse and Danes; the Goths get a Gothic unit, for example lance cavalry like that at Adrianople (378);
- Mounted Trebuchet to the Tanguts;
- Rocket Cart removed from the Khitans and Jurchens; the Fire Lancer can stay with the Khitans as stretched but acceptable;
- Gbeto replaced by a Mandinka unit based on al-Umari and Ibn Battuta;
- Woad Raider replaced by Kern or Gallowglass;
- Arambai explicitly linked to the “Cassay Horse”, or replaced by a Pagan unit;
- the Huns' Atheism replaced by something with a historical basis, for example the Roman tribute (the 2,100 pounds of gold a year under the treaty of 447).
The Chu Ko Nu can stay with the Chinese, because the zhuge nu is documented in the Ming period.

Rewrite the 1999–2015 history texts using current historiography. Most urgent corrections:
- Celts: Stonehenge and “Northern Europe”;
- Aztecs: “invented little” and the chronology of the epidemics;
- Maya: “simple” religion, four codices, causes of the collapse;
- Berbers: the Alhambra and the Mosque of Córdoba;
- Persians: Ctesiphon is not Baghdad; Yazdegerd III died in 651;
- Turks: the Great Seljuk state falls in 1194; Saladin was not a Seljuk;
- Vikings: the name does not mean “northmen”; the siege of Paris was in 885–886; Baldwin I was not a Norman; there were raids after the year 1000;
- Goths: Alaric sacked Rome in 410; the Gauls had already done so;
- Saxons: Ælle of Sussex, Middlesex, the shield wall, Æthelstan in 927;
- Huns: the stirrup;
- Mapuche: “largest group in South America”;
- Tupi: Staden was a captive in 1554.

Apply the name review to the translations as well. In Portuguese, for example:
- “Ingleses” instead of “Bretões”;
- “Muíscas” instead of “Chibchas”;
- “Varegues” instead of “Varangianos”;
- “Gurjaras” without the article;
- a note on “Tártaros”.
As an exercise for the community, propose in the repository an alternative data file (“roster-historico.json”) where each player can test their own roster principle and see the regional gaps calculated automatically.

findings.detail:
Several decisions show real research and should serve as a model for any proposal:
- no Stable in the Americas, and Mapuche cavalry only via the Castle (faithful to the 16th century);
- the Three Kingdoms' Traction Trebuchet;
- the Indian Armored and Siege Elephant in place of the ram;
- the Khmer Ballista Elephant;
- the Varangian Guard, with the Saxons included;
- the Korean Rocket Cart (hwacha) and the Jurchen line of fire lances and bombs;
- the Steppe Lancer restricted to Cumans, Jurchens, Khitans, Mongols and Tatars, the lance-wielding peoples of the steppes and their fringes;
- the Persian Savar and the Parthian Tactics available earlier;
- the Flaming Camel;
- the UT Ballistas and the Roman Comitatenses;
- the Hunnic Siege Ram (Priscus) and the Bulgarian Siege Onager (Krum);
- the Aztec Gambesons (ichcahuipilli);
- Wu's Fast Fire Ship and UT Red Cliffs Tactics;
- the Eagle Warrior restricted to Aztecs and Mayans;
- the Polish-Lithuanian Winged Hussar (late, 16th and 17th centuries, but consistent).

The pattern is clear: the successes almost always come from replacement or regional units, and the errors come from the generic model distributed for balance.
